<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">War and march · 1933 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Polyushko-pole</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="ru">Полюшко-поле</bdi> / <bdi lang="ru-Latn">Polyushko-pole</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Soviet song from 1933 about a young soldier setting out across the wide plains, later played by jazz and rock bands around the world.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Viktor Gusev</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Lev Knipper</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>the Soviet Union</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Russian</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Soviet Union</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1933</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>War and march</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>In copyright (words by Viktor Gusev, music by Lev Knipper (1933))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polyushko-pole">Polyushko-pole</a>&quot;, known in English as &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> of the Plains&quot;, &quot;Meadowlands&quot;, &quot;Cavalry of the Steppes&quot; or &quot;O Fields, My Fields&quot;, is a Soviet song with music by Lev Knipper and words by Viktor Gusev, written in 1933. In Russian pole means a plain or field, and polyushko is its fond, diminutive form. The song became part of Knipper's symphony with chorus &quot;A Poem about a Komsomol Soldier&quot;, composed in 1934 to words by Gusev. Its words are sung in the voice of a young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> recruit.</p><p>The song was performed many times in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. The Alexandrov Ensemble, best known abroad as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> Choir, has sung and recorded it regularly, and the group Poyushchiye Gitary released a well-known rock version around 1967. At the opening of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> Youth Congress of 1945 a choir of 6,000 sang the whole song.</p><p>It was already known in the West: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-robeson">Paul Robeson</a> recorded it in 1942 as &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> of the Plains&quot;, in English and Russian, on his album Songs of Free Men (1943). Later arrangements include the Swedish jazz pianist Jan Johansson's &quot;Stepp, min stepp&quot; (1967), Jefferson Airplane's instrumental &quot;Meadowlands&quot; on Volunteers (1969), Phillip Bimstein's &quot;Meadowlands&quot; (1982), a version by James Last, and, as &quot;The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> Patrol&quot;, a version by Ivan Rebroff. The Rahbani brothers reused the melody, crediting Knipper, for &quot;Kanou Ya Habibi&quot;, sung by Fairuz in their 1974 play Loulou, and Chabad-Lubavitch Hasidim made a nigun of it.</p><p>The tune has been used in film and television: in the opening credits of The Russians Are Coming, the Russians Are Coming (1966), throughout Reds (1981), at the start of Cast Away, in the film Comrade Pedersen, where schoolchildren sing it in class, in Airwolf, in the anime Girls und Panzer, in the series Kleo, and in the Russian film Captain Volkonogov Escaped (2021). Michael Palin sang it with the choir of the Russian Pacific Fleet in Full Circle with Michael Palin, and bands from Blackmore's Night (1999) to Powerwolf (2009) have set their own songs to it.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A soldier's song of a young recruit leaving home to stand guard for his country; proud and earnest in mood.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-nowords sg-nowords-g" data-say>The words of this song are still in copyright, so the Institute does not print them itself: Genius shows them below, under its own licence.</p><div class="sg-genius"><div class="sg-genius-btns"><button type="button" class="sg-genius-btn" data-genius="8528458" data-what="Lyrics">Show the lyrics from Genius</button></div><div class="sg-genius-frames"></div><p class="sg-genius-note">Genius shows the lyrics in its own frame, under its own licence: nothing loads from Genius until you press a button. <a href="https://genius.com/Shortparis-polyushko-pole-lyrics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The song on Genius</a>.</p></div></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="N73QOe2t-a4" data-title="Polyushko-pole: the Alexandrov Ensemble"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">the Alexandrov Ensemble</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel The Red Army Choir - Topic. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N73QOe2t-a4" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">A Poem about a Komsomol Soldier (Lev Knipper, symphony with chorus)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1934</span><span class="sg-chain-h">part of</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Song of the Plains (Paul Robeson, Songs of Free Men)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1942</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recorded in English and Russian</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Stepp, min stepp (Jan Johansson, Jazz på ryska)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1967</span><span class="sg-chain-h">jazz arrangement</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Rock version (Poyushchiye Gitary)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">c. 1967</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adapted as</span></li><li><a href="/songs/meadowlands">Meadowlands (Jefferson Airplane, Volunteers)</a> <span class="sg-chain-y">1969</span><span class="sg-chain-h">instrumental version</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Kanou Ya Habibi (Rahbani brothers, sung by Fairuz in Loulou)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1974</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody</span></li><li><a href="/songs/meadowlands">Meadowlands (Phillip Bimstein; Phil 'N' the Blanks, Lands and Peoples)</a> <span class="sg-chain-y">1982</span><span class="sg-chain-h">arrangement</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Cossack Patrol (Ivan Rebroff)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">arrangement</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">James Last, In Russia</span><span class="sg-chain-h">instrumental version</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Gone with the Wind (Blackmore's Night, Under a Violet Moon)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1999</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Werewolves of Armenia (Powerwolf, Bible of the Beast)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2009</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Der Rebbe Zal Zayn Gezunt (Chabad-Lubavitch nigun)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody, new words</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Hold Me Forever (Ervin and Andrea Litkei)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">based on the melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Russians Are Coming, the Russians Are Coming (film)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1966</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in (opening credits)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Reds (film)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1981</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Cast Away (film)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Comrade Pedersen (film)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Captain Volkonogov Escaped (film)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2021</span><span class="sg-chain-h">theme song</span></li></ul></section>
